{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929","id":"msg_01VZPeco8Mbk2SCrwpT836y8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent! I can see the problem now. In the SQL log, we can see:\n1. The UPDATE statement is NEVER executed\n2. Only SELECT statements are issued during save\n3. The update is not being written to the database\n\nThe problem is that we call `session.get(SignalChain, chain.id)` which loads the ORM object, but we're not actually committing the changes to that object! The updates to `existing.name`, `existing.description`, etc. are modifying the ORM object, but since `expire_on_commit=False`, those changes are lost after commit.\n\nActually, wait - looking at the SQL log, there's NO UPDATE statement at all. The issue is that we update the ORM object fields (`existing.name = chain.name`) but we don't mark it as dirty or flush it. Actually, SQLAlchemy should automatically track those changes... unless the object is already in a detached or expired state.\n\nLet me look more closely. The issue is after calling `await self.session.get(SignalChain, chain.id)`, we get an ORM object and update its fields. But the session already knows about this object from the first save, and `expire_on_commit=False` means it doesn't expire the object after commit. So when we update the fields, SQLAlchemy might not detect the changes.\n\nLet me try adding `session.add(existing)` after updating the fields:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11857,"cache_read_input_tokens":125090,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11857},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50b63515-2767-4b73-ade9-cf678352b806","uuid":"ff443ade-bb7d-4c71-8a36-6ab1b46ad2a5"}
